<h2>Two variations</h2>
<p><code>im-poster-icon-paper</code> keeps the white ground of every other type and puts the series colour into the glyph — for a channel that mixes icon thumbnails with photo ones and wants them to sit together. <code>im-poster-icon-side</code> moves the glyph left and gives the words the right half, for the rare icon video whose title is the hook.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-grid-auto im-grid-md">
	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-icon im-poster-icon-paper" style="--im-poster-tint: var(--im-c-snippet)"><span class="im-poster-chip">snippets</span><span class="im-poster-ep">#18</span><span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "terminal"}}</span><strong class="im-poster-word">one-line deploys</strong></figure>
	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-icon im-poster-icon-side"><span class="im-poster-chip">field notes</span><span class="im-poster-ep">EP 05</span><span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "flame"}}</span><strong class="im-poster-word">burnout is a design bug</strong></figure>
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>Don't</h2>
<ul>
	<li>Put a brand's logo in place of the glyph. It is somebody else's mark, and at 168px every logo looks like every other.</li>
	<li>Mix icon sets, or outline and filled glyphs, in one channel.</li>
	<li>Use an icon thumbnail when there <em>is</em> a face or a place. The icon is the fallback, not the shortcut.</li>
	<li>Give two series the same colour. The colour is the series' name.</li>
</ul>
